 "rulebook": "The Asian Jump Rope Union's AC2026 Asian Long Rope Championship Rules (AJRU, IJRU's continental body): event #2 '8 Jumpers' (two turners turn one long rope, the jumpers jump it together; the rope must pass under the feet of the jumpers to score 1 point) with the turners 3.6 m apart, the distance event #1 'Figure 8' sets (event #2 sets none); the common rules (the rope remains still until the start signal; leaving the event area is a space violation); and the IJRU Judging Manual (show freestyle, long rope): a long rope is at least 6.0 m long",
 "deviations": [
  "8 Jumpers: one jumper (the robot) instead of eight, and a count of consecutive jumps, 10 in a row with no miss, instead of the jumps in 1 minute; the count is the benchmark's qualifying mark, a round number (school long-rope practice counts runs of consecutive jumps; no rule book sets this one)",
  "the turners are scripted, not athletes: each holds the rope 1.4 m from its end, the rest wound round the hand, so that the free rope between the hands (3.2 m) just brushes the floor under the jumper (as turners shorten a long rope to the jumpers); each moves the hand on a vertical circle of 0.4 m radius whose centre is 1.0 m high and 0.4 m in front of the turner, at a steady 1.0 revolutions per second; they never adapt to the jumper, and they do not stop when the rope is caught (the rope's ends beyond the hands are not modelled)",
  "the start: the rope is already turning at the start (t = 0), not still until a signal; its lowest part has just passed under the robot, going backwards",
  "the end: the event has none (the clock stops the round); here the robot must land on its feet after the last jump and stand still for 0.5 s, all before the still-turning rope comes round again (a longer stand would need the turners to stop the rope, and a stopping rope swings on into the jumper)",
  "a miss is any touch of the rope on the robot (the rules only score the passes); a jump is a pass of the rope under both feet while nothing of the robot touches the ground, and the robot must stay in the middle of the rope (the centre zone) instead of the event's space rule",
  "the rope: a 0.01 m cotton rope of 0.06 kg/m (a typical 10 mm braided cotton rope) modelled as a chain of 40 short capsules joined end to end by stiff point constraints (free to bend and twist: a cord has almost no bending stiffness), in still air with its drag; no wind"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Jump the long rope: standing in the middle of a rope turned by two turners, jump it 10 times in a row, the rope passing under both your feet while you are in the air, without the rope ever touching you.",
  "rules": "Two turners stand 3.6 m apart, one on each side of you (on the y axis, at y = -1.8 and +1.8 m); they turn a 6.0 m rope, 0.01 m thick, holding it 1.4 m from each end, at a steady 1.0 revolutions per second: each hand goes round a vertical circle of 0.4 m radius, 1.0 m above the floor, 0.4 m in front of its turner. The rope goes over your head from behind you to the front, down in front of you and under you towards your back; it is already turning at the start. A jump counts when the rope passes under both your feet while nothing of you touches the ground (at the moment it passes under each foot), after it has passed over your head since the last counted jump (one jump per turn of the rope). You succeed when, after 10 counted jumps, the first contact with the floor is your feet only and you then stand still for 0.5 s (only your feet on the floor, your torso within about 20 degrees of vertical, your pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s): the turners keep turning, so this has to be done before the rope comes round again, and it must not touch you until then. You fail at once if the rope touches any part of you (a miss: the turners do not stop), if your pelvis leaves the centre zone (within 0.5 m of the rope's axis forwards and backwards and 0.5 m of the middle between the turners sideways), if the rope passes under a foot that is on the ground or through you, on a fall (your pelvis lower than 0.35 m, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground), on a landing that is not on the feet after the last jump, or after 20 s.",
  "venue": "You stand facing +x on a wooden gym floor, in the middle between two turners, who stand at y = -1.8 m and y = +1.8 m on the line through your feet (you face across the rope). The rope's axis, the line between the centres of the turners' hand circles, is 1.0 m above the floor right above your feet. The centre zone is painted on the floor. At the start the rope is turning at 1.0 revolutions per second; its lowest part has just passed under you and is rising behind you: it comes over your head, down in front of you and under your feet once per turn.",
  "difficulty": "Neither built-in gait can jump: the robot must hop again and again, in time with a rope it has to watch, with its feet off the ground at the very moment the rope passes and back on balance before the next turn. Each landing must be absorbed while the next take-off is already being prepared, and any drift out of the middle or a toe left behind catches the rope."
 },
